<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sasol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sasol is a South African chemicals and energy company founded by the state in 1950 to make fuel from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, using the Fischer–Tropsch process at Sasolburg and later at Secunda. Its synthetic fuels reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s dependence on imported oil during the apartheid-era <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>. Privatised from 1979, it remains one of the country's largest companies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sasol.jpg" alt="Sasol" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tom Corser · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sasol_Corporate_Office_Johannesburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> has large deposits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> whose high fly ash content gave it little commercial value, and from the 1920s South African scientists studied turning it into liquid fuels. P. N. Lategan of the Transvaal Coal Owners Association pioneered the work, and a government white paper of 1927 described the oil-from-coal processes used overseas. After the Second World War Anglovaal bought the rights to a Fischer–Tropsch method patented by M. W. Kellogg Limited, and in 1950 the company was incorporated as the South African Coal, Oil and Gas Corporation, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> name gives the abbreviation Sasol. Etienne Rousseau, a scientist trained at the University of Stellenbosch, was its first managing director.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Commissioning of the Sasol 1 plant began in 1954. Its first reactor of 1955, a circulating fluidised bed with a capacity of about 1,500 barrels a day, was improved to about 6,500, and later fixed fluidised bed and slurry phase reactors raised capacities further. Sasol 2 was completed in 1980 and Sasol 3 came on stream in 1982. The Sigma mine in Sasolburg began with two million tons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> a year, and its output peaked at 7.4 million tons in 1991.</p><p>To lessen its exposure to the oil price, Sasol moved into chemicals made in the Fischer–Tropsch process: ammonia, styrene and butadiene in the 1960s, nitrogen fertilisers by 1964, phosphate and potassium fertilisers in the 1980s. The Natref refinery at Sasolburg, a joint venture commissioned in 1971, received 70% of its oil from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> by 1979, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a>. Abroad, the Oryx gas-to-liquids plant in Qatar opened in 2007 with QatarEnergy, and a cracker complex was built at Lake Charles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, whose overrun of $4 billion led the joint chief executives to resign in October 2019.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-to-liquids process gives some of the highest greenhouse gas emissions per unit of product in the world: in 2020 the Secunda plant was the largest single point source of greenhouse gas, at 56.5 million tonnes of carbon dioxide a year. In 2008 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> fined Sasol €318 million for taking part in a paraffin wax cartel. The company is listed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> stock exchanges and is based in Sandton.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sasol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy grew fast in the 1960s on gold, other minerals and manufacturing, supported by state corporations in steel, electricity and synthetic fuels such as <b>Sasol</b>, and the high gold price of the 1970s brought further income, but growth slowed in the 1980s under sanctions, strikes and debt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
